  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q25-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "25",
    "prompt": "A 2-litre mixture contains water and cordial in the ratio $3:4$. How many litres of water must be added to change the ratio to $4:3$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{2}{3}$ litre.\n\nKeep the cordial volume fixed and use the new water-to-cordial ratio.",
      "worked": "The original volumes are\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Water} &= \\frac{3}{7}\\times2=\\frac{6}{7}\\text{ L}\\\\\\text{Cordial} &= \\frac{4}{7}\\times2=\\frac{8}{7}\\text{ L}.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $x$ litres of water be added. The cordial volume stays unchanged.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+\\frac{6}{7}}{\\frac{8}{7}} &= \\frac{4}{3}\\\\3\\left(x+\\frac{6}{7}\\right) &= 4\\times\\frac{8}{7}\\\\3x+\\frac{18}{7} &= \\frac{32}{7}\\\\21x+18 &= 32\\\\21x &= 14\\\\x &= \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nExactly $\\frac{2}{3}$ litre of water must be added.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
